# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DO NOT COMMIT SOURCE EXPORTS.
#
# The raw Orders_and_Appointments export carries member IDs and authorization
# numbers inside the "Bot Run Fail Reason" text. This repository is public, so
# committing one would publish HIPAA identifiers irreversibly (crawlers and
# caches retain content even after a force-push or repo deletion).
#
# Only docs/data.json is publishable: it holds pre-aggregated counts with the
# identifiers stripped. Build it with Build-Dashboard.ps1.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.xlsx
*.xls
*.xlsm
*.csv

# Sanitized row-level copies. Safe by construction, but they are still
# row-level data and there is no reason to publish them - the dashboard is
# driven entirely by docs/data.json. (Also matched by *.csv above; listed
# explicitly so the intent survives a future change to that rule.)
sanitized/

# The hard-schedule provider roster. Real provider names, same PPI class as the
# coordinator names the build discards at ingest - and this repo is public. The
# build reads it to label Appointment rows "Easy"/"Hard" and publishes only those
# two words, so the roster itself never needs to leave this machine.
providers.hard.txt

# The dashboard passphrase. This is the key that decrypts docs/data.json, so
# committing it to a public repo would publish the lock and the key together and
# undo the whole access gate. Same sidecar pattern as providers.hard.txt: the
# build reads it, the published output only ever carries ciphertext derived from
# it. Rotating it means editing this file and re-running the build.
dashboard.passphrase.txt

# Single-file dashboards from Build-Standalone.ps1. These carry the aggregates
# INLINE AND IN PLAINTEXT, with no passphrase prompt, because they are built to
# be opened from disk where there is no server to fetch data.json. That makes
# them the one generated artifact that must never be committed: pushing one here
# would publish every figure in it to a public URL and reduce the access gate on
# docs/data.json to decoration. Share them through a channel that already
# controls access instead.
standalone/

# Local scratch
_upload/
_shots/
_dashboard_build/
_installers/
*.log
*.tmp

# Editor / OS noise
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/

# Claude Code local settings
.claude/settings.local.json

# Explicitly re-allow the only generated data file that is safe to publish.
!docs/data.json
